23 years ago at the age of 19 I shot and  killed a man I was a young drug dealer  with a quick temper and a semi-automatic  pistol but that wasn't the end of my  story in fact it was the beginning in  the 23 years since there's a story of  acknowledgement apology and atonement  but it didn't happen in the way that you  might imagine or think these things  occurred in my life in the way that was  surprising especially to me see like any  of you growing up I was an honor roll  student scholarship student but dreams  of becoming a doctor but things went  dramatically wrong my parents separated  and eventually divorced the actual  events are pretty straightforward at the  age of 17 I got shot three times  stunned on the corner of my block in  Detroit my friend rushed me to the  hospital doctors pulled the bullets out  hats me up and send me back to the same  neighborhood I got shot throughout this  ordeal  no one hugged me no one counseled me no  one told me I would be okay no one told  me that I would live in fear that I will  become paranoid but that I would react  hyper violently to being shot no one  told me that one day I would become the  person behind the trigger 14 months  later  at 2:00 a.m. I fired the shots that  caused the man's death when I answered  prison I was bitter I was angry I was  hurt I didn't want to take  responsibility I blamed everybody for my  parents to the system  I rest in lies my decision to shoot  because in the hood where I come from  it's better be the shooter than the  person getting shot as I said in my cold  cell I felt helpless unloved and  abandoned I felt like nobody cared and I  reacted with hostility to my confinement  and I found myself getting deeper in the  deeper in trouble  I ran black market stores a loan sharks  and I saw drugs that was legally  smuggled into the prison I had in fact  become what the war done a Miskin  reformatory called the worst of the  worse and because of my activity I  landed in solitary confinement for seven  and a half years out of my incarceration  now as I see a solitary confinement is  one of the most inhumane and barbaric  places you can find yourself well find  myself I did one day was pacing myself  when the officer came and delivered mail  I looked at a couple of letters before I  looked at the letter that had my son's  Quigley handwriting on it and any time I  would get this letter from my son it was  like a ray of light in the darkest place  you can imagine and on this particular  day I opened this letter and in capital  letters he wrote my momma told me why he  was in prison murder  said dad don't kill Jesus watches what  you do pray to Him now I wasn't  religious at that time normal religious  now but it was something so profound  about my son's words they made me  examine things about my life that I  hadn't considered it was the first time  in my life that I had actually thought  about the fact that my son would see me  as a murderer I sat back on my bunk and  I reflected on something I had read in  Plato's Republic where Socrates stated  an apology that the unexamined life  isn't worth living at that point is when  the transformation began but it didn't  come easy  one of the things I realized was part of  the transformation is that there are  four key things the first thing was I  had great mentors now I know some of  y'all probably thinking like how do you  find a great mentor in prison but in my  case some of my mentors were serving  life sentences were some of the best  people to ever come into my life because  they forced me to look at my life  honestly and they forced me to challenge  myself about my decision-making the  second thing was literature prior to  going to prison I didn't know that there  were so many brilliant black poets  authors and philosophers and then I had  the great fortune of encountering  Malcolm X's autobiography and it  shattered every stereotype I had about  myself the third thing was family for 19  years my father stood by my side with an  unshakable faith because he believed it  I had what it took to turn my life  around I also met an amazing woman who  was not a mother my two-year-old son say  cool she taught me how to love myself in  a healthy way  the final thing was writing when I got  that letter for my son I began to write  a journal about things I had experienced  in my childhood and in prison and what  it did is it opened up my mind to the  idea of atonement earlier in my  incarceration I received a letter from  one of the relatives of my victim and in  that letter she told me she forgave me  because she realized I was a young child  who had been abused and I've been  through some hardships and just made a  series of poor decisions  it was the first time in my life that I  ever felt open to forgiving myself one  of the things that happened after that  experience is that I thought about the  other men who were incarcerated long  side of me and how much I wanted to  share this with them and so I started  talking to them about some of their  experiences and I was devastated to  realize that most of them came from the  same abusive environments and most of  them wanted to help and they wanted to  turn that around  but unfortunately the system that  currently holds 2.5 million people in  prison is designed a warehouse as  opposed to rehabilitate or transformed  so I made it up in my mind that if I was  ever released from prison that I would  do everything in my power to help change  that 2010 I walked out of prison for the  first time after two decades now imagine  if you will Fred Flintstone  walking into an episode of the Jetsons  that was pretty much what my life was  like for the first time I was exposed to  the internet social media cars that  talked like hit from the Nightrider but  the thing that fascinated me the most  was phone technology see when I went to  prison our car phones was this big and  cry like two people to carry him so  imagine what it was like when I first  grabbed my little blackberry and I  started learning how to text but the  thing is the people around me they  didn't realize that I had no idea what  all these abbreviated sex men like lol  oMG lmao until one day I was having a  conversation with one of my friends via  text and I asked him to do something he  responded back cake and I was like what  is K and he was like k is okay so in my  head I was like well what's the hell is  wrong okay  and so I take some a question mark and  he said K equals okay it's luck setback  fu and then he checks back and he asked  me what was our custom amount and I said  lol fu as in I finally understand and so  fast for three years I'm doing  relatively good I have a fellowship at  MIT Media Lab I work for an amazing  company called beat me I teach at the  University of Michigan but it's been a  struggle because I realized that there  are more men and women coming home who  are not going to be afforded those  opportunities I've been blessed to work  with some amazing men and women helping  others reenter society one of them is my  friend named Calvin Evans he started to  serve 24 years for crime he didn't  commit  45 years old it's currently enrolled in  college  and one of the things that we talked  about is the three things that I found  important of my personal transformation  the first being acknowledgment  I had the knowledge that I had heard of  us I also had the knowledge that I had  been hurt the second thing was  apologizing I had to apologize to the  people I hurt even though I had no  expectations of them accepting it it was  important to do because it was the right  thing but also had the pologize to  myself the third thing was atoning for  me atoning meant going back it's from  our community and working with at-risk  youth who were on the same path but also  becoming that one with myself through my  experience being locked up one of the  things I discovered is this the majority  of men and women who are incarcerated  are redeemable and the fact is 90% of  the men and women were incarcerated but  at some point returned to the community  and we have a role in determining what  kind of men and women return to our  community my wish today is that we will  embrace a more empathetic approach to  how we deal with mass incarceration  that we would do away with the lock them  up and throw away to keep mentality  because it's proven it doesn't work my  journey is a unique journey but it  doesn't have to be that way anybody can  have transformation if we create the  space for that to happen so what I'm  asking today is that to envision a world  where men and women aren't held hostage  to their past where misdeeds and  mistakes doesn't define you for the rest  of your life I think collectively we can  create that reality and I hope you do  too  thank you  [Applause]
 

